\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



Put $A(X)=|A\cap[1,X]|$ and $\mathcal P=\{p:p\text{ prime}\}$. An eventual additive complement to the primes satisfies $[N_0,\infty)\cap{\mathbb{N}}\subseteq\mathcal P+A$ for some $N_0$. The set $A$ is fixed while $X$ grows; choosing a different complement for each cutoff would be a weaker problem. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.erdosproblems.com/32}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is there a set $A\subset\mathbb{N}$ such that $ \lvert A\cap\{1,\ldots,N\}\rvert = o((\log N)^2) $ and such that every large integer can be written as $p+a$ for some prime $p$ and $a\in A$?
Can the bound $O(\log N)$ be achieved? Must such an $A$ satisfy $ \liminf \frac{\lvert A\cap\{1,\ldots,N\}\rvert}{\log N}> 1? $ \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Scope and interpretation.}

The supplied background reports the lower-limit inequality as a proved subpart. The sparse-complement construction and the sharper logarithmic size remain the questions retained here. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Decide whether an additive complement to the primes can have A(x)=o((log x)\textasciicircum{}2), especially O(log x), while covering every sufficiently large integer. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

{\small\emph{Transcription note.} Only unambiguous escape/formatting damage and nonmathematical serialization debris were repaired in the displayed source statement.}

\subsection{Short English statement}
How thin can an integer set be while every sufficiently large integer is a prime plus an element of that set?

